This is a port of Quake, originally created by webOS Internals. Updated in 2026 by codepoet and webOS Archive to add Bluetooth and USB Controller Support.

http://www.libsdl.org/projects/quake/

The source code is from http://www.libsdl.org/projects/quake/src/sdlquake-1.0.9.tar.gz


================================================================================
CONTROLS
================================================================================

Three input methods work at once: the on-screen touch overlay, a USB or
Bluetooth game controller, and a USB or Bluetooth keyboard. Plug a controller or
keyboard in at any time -- they are picked up within about a second, with no
need to restart the game.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
KEYBOARD
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

These are Quake 1.09's own stock bindings. NOTE: there is no WASD -- movement is
on the arrow keys, which is how Quake shipped in 1996. See "Rebinding" below if
you want WASD.

  Movement
    Up Arrow / Down Arrow      Move forward / backward
    Left Arrow / Right Arrow   Turn left / right
    Alt (hold) + Left/Right    Strafe instead of turn
    , and .                    Strafe left / right
    Shift (hold)               Run
    Space or Enter             Jump
    D / C                      Swim up / down

  Combat
    Ctrl                       Fire
    1 - 8                      Select weapon directly
    /                          Next weapon

  Looking
    A / Z                      Look up / down
    Page Down / Delete         Look up / down
    End                        Centre the view
    Insert (hold)              Keyboard look
    \ (hold)                   Mouse look

  Interface
    Esc                        Menu
    ` (backtick)               Console
    Tab                        Scoreboard
    T                          Chat message
    Pause                      Pause
    - / =                      Shrink / enlarge the view
    F1                         Help
    F2 / F3                    Save / Load menu
    F4                         Options menu
    F5                         Multiplayer menu
    F6 / F9                    Quicksave / Quickload
    F10                        Quit
    F11                        Zoom in
    F12                        Screenshot

Every printable key, arrow, function key and modifier reaches the game, and
holding Shift produces capitals and shifted symbols when typing in the console.
Verified with a Mitsumi/Apple Extended USB keyboard; a Bluetooth keyboard
paired through webOS should work the same way, as it arrives over the same
/dev/input path.

Rebinding: open the console with ` and use Quake's normal bind command, for
example

    bind w +forward
    bind s +back
    bind a +moveleft
    bind d +moveright

Bindings are saved to id1/config.cfg and persist across launches.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
GAME CONTROLLER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

One scheme is used for every supported pad, so muscle memory carries between
them. Positions below are described physically; CONTROLS.md gives the exact
button for each pad, and CONTROLS.csv is the machine-readable table.

  In game
    Left stick, D-pad          Move and strafe
    Right stick                Look
    Face buttons               Look left / down / right / up
                               (so aiming works on pads with no right stick)
    Left shoulder              Jump
    Right shoulder             Fire
    Left trigger               Run (hold)
    Right trigger              Next weapon
    Stick clicks               Run
    Start                      Menu
    Back / Select              Previous weapon

  In menus
    D-pad, left stick          Navigate
    Right side of the pad      Select
      (face-down, face-right, right shoulder/trigger/stick-click)
    Left side of the pad       Back
      (face-left, face-up, left shoulder/trigger/stick-click, Back, Start)

No in-game action fires while a menu is open.

Verified pads: Sony DualShock 4 (USB and Bluetooth), ShanWan "Xbox knock-off"
wireless, DragonRise "Sega Saturn style" wireless, and the Logitech Precision
Gamepad. Other pads fall back to a generic profile that follows the standard
evdev button names and should work without configuration.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TOUCH
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The touch overlay has three zones. Only two of them are drawn, which is the
single biggest source of confusion -- read "Jump" below before deciding it is
broken.

  Zone            Where                        Drawn?
  --------------  ---------------------------  ---------------------------------
  Move stick      Bottom-LEFT, 320x320 px      Yes, always
  Fire            Bottom-RIGHT, 320x320 px     Yes, always
  Jump            TOP strip, full width,       No -- see below
                  top 240 px of the screen

  Move            Press the on-screen stick and drag. It is analog: how far you
                  drag is how fast you move. There is a small dead zone at the
                  centre so resting your thumb does not creep.
  Fire            Tap or hold anywhere in the bottom-right zone. Holding
                  auto-fires.

  Jump            *** Slide, do not tap. ***
                  Put a finger anywhere along the top of the screen and MOVE it
                  slightly. A perfectly stationary tap does nothing at all.

QUIRK 1 -- the jump button is invisible, and it needs a slide.

  There is a jump graphic (images/jump.png), but it is not a button you press.
  It flashes at the top-centre of the screen for a few frames AFTER a successful
  jump, purely as confirmation. So the control you are looking for is not drawn
  anywhere: the whole top 240 px of the screen, edge to edge, is the jump zone.

  The zone only responds to finger MOVEMENT, not to a clean tap. Internally the
  jump fires from SDL_MOUSEMOTION, and a tap that never moves emits no motion
  event. Touch the top of the screen and slide a little and it jumps every time.

QUIRK 2 -- to get from the opening demo into the menu, tap near the TOP of the
screen (usually twice).

  There is no dedicated "open menu" touch gesture. What actually happens is a
  side effect worth knowing, because it explains the double tap:

    1. Your tap lands in the invisible jump zone -- and the Quake logo and menu
       sit high on the screen, so aiming at "where the menu should be" tends to
       put your finger in the top 240 px without meaning to.
    2. Jump sends the Space key.
    3. While the attract-mode demo is playing, Quake opens the menu on ANY
       printable keypress (keys.c: `if (cls.demoplayback && down &&
       consolekeys[key] && key_dest == key_game) M_ToggleMenu_f();`), and Space
       counts. So the jump gesture opens the menu.

  The first tap often does nothing because it did not move enough to emit a
  motion event; the second tap lands a few pixels off the first and does. Hence
  "double tap". A single tap-and-slide along the top edge works the first time.

  Note this trick only works during the demo. Once you are actually in a level,
  Space just jumps -- see the limitation below.

Once a menu IS open, the overlay is reinterpreted so a touch-only player can
navigate it:

  Fire zone (bottom-right)   Enter / Select
  Top strip                  Escape (back out, or close the menu)
  Move stick (bottom-left)   Arrow keys -- one step per push, re-arming when you
                             return the stick toward centre

LIMITATION -- you cannot open the menu by touch during gameplay.

  In a live game there is no touch gesture for Escape; the top strip is jump.
  To reach the menu mid-game (to save, load, or quit) you need one of:

    Game controller   Start
    Keyboard          Escape

  This is a real gap for touch-only players rather than an intentional design,
  and it is the first thing to fix if you are extending the overlay.

The overlay can be hidden entirely with orange + '@' on a hardware keyboard.
There is no touch gesture for this.
